WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.117
In this code example, we import three built-in tools: math, random, and date from datetime.

00:00:05.117 --> 00:00:11.302
The math module gives us mathematical functions and constants.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:15.227
We use math.sqrt to calculate the square root of 81.

00:00:15.227 --> 00:00:19.297
We also use math.pi and round it to three decimal places.

00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:22.917
The random module gives us tools for random selection.

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:25.902
In this example, random.sample chooses three sample IDs from a range of numbers.

00:00:25.902 --> 00:00:33.847
This is a useful pattern when you want to select a few items from a larger group.

00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:37.572
The date object from datetime lets us work with dates.

00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:40.827
We use date.today to get the current date.

00:00:40.827 --> 00:00:44.097
Before running the code, predict what you expect.

00:00:44.097 --> 00:00:46.877
The square root of 81 should be 9.

00:00:46.877 --> 00:00:51.032
The rounded value of pi should be close to 3.142.

00:00:51.032 --> 00:00:55.267
The sample IDs may change each time because they are selected randomly.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:57.577
The date should show today's date.

00:00:57.577 --> 00:01:02.882
After running the cell, try changing the number from 81 to 144.

00:01:02.882 --> 00:01:05.992
Then run it again and check the new square root.

00:01:05.992 --> 00:01:10.642
The main lesson is that Python already includes many useful modules.

00:01:10.642 --> 00:01:15.002
Imports allow us to use those tools without writing everything ourselves.
